1
00:00:03,350 --> 00:00:08,246
In the SQL Editor, on eth lake, list the databases this connection has attached.

2
00:00:08,526 --> 00:00:17,140
Two matter: eth lake, the lake, and underscore underscore ducklake metadata eth lake: the catalog file, as a plain DuckDB database.

3
00:00:18,090 --> 00:00:20,113
Its tables all start with ducklake.

4
00:00:20,393 --> 00:00:23,456
These are the tables the DuckLake specification defines.

5
00:00:23,736 --> 00:00:26,501
Nothing else: the whole lake's metadata is in them.

6
00:00:27,451 --> 00:00:31,362
Ducklake snapshot has one row per snapshot, with its schema version.

7
00:00:31,642 --> 00:00:35,674
Six and seven are missing: we expired them in Lesson 4.4.

8
00:00:36,624 --> 00:00:43,590
Ducklake snapshot changes says what each snapshot did: the November file we added, the merge, the inlining flush.

9
00:00:43,870 --> 00:00:46,028
This is what Studio's History tab shows.

10
00:00:46,308 --> 00:00:48,497
A commit message would be stored here too.

11
00:00:49,447 --> 00:00:54,537
Ducklake table: one row per table, with an id, and the snapshot it was created in.

12
00:00:54,817 --> 00:00:59,532
Begin snapshot, end snapshot: a table that was dropped gets an end snapshot.

13
00:01:00,482 --> 00:01:03,621
And ducklake column, for column three of the blocks table.

14
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:10,169
Two rows: miner, valid from snapshot two until fourteen, and fee recipient from fourteen on.

15
00:01:10,449 --> 00:01:12,730
That is the rename from Lesson 3.3.

16
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:16,705
Same id, new name, and the old name is kept for time travel.

17
00:01:17,655 --> 00:01:19,684
That is the whole trick of the catalog.

18
00:01:19,964 --> 00:01:23,100
Every row has a begin snapshot and an end snapshot.

19
00:01:23,380 --> 00:01:29,847
The lake at snapshot s is simply every row where begin is at most s, and end is after s, or empty.

20
00:01:30,797 --> 00:01:36,321
Save the queries as 5 underscore 1 catalog tables dot sequel, commit, and push.

21
00:01:58,770 --> 00:02:02,682
To recap: the catalog is a plain database of ducklake tables.

22
00:02:02,962 --> 00:02:08,406
Snapshots, tables and columns are rows, and begin and end snapshot version all of them.

23
00:02:08,686 --> 00:02:11,702
Next: the files, their statistics, and partitions.
